id: guide
title: Tutorial and Guide
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"tutorial-and-guide","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tutorial and guide"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Workflow is a headless durable execution system for TypeScript. You\nwrite workflows as ordinary async functions, mark durable work with explicit\nprimitives, and choose where persistence, timers, schedules, and deployment\nlive."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The production model is deliberately app-centered. The workflow engine does not\nbecome a separate platform where your business logic lives. Your app starts and\nresumes runs, your store records durability, and your host wakes bounded work\nwhen timers or schedules are due."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This guide walks through the production shape:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Define a workflow with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-core"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Put it behind a runtime with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-runtime"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persist executions in a durable store."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Observe runtime, store, and step execution with OpenTelemetry."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wake due timers and schedules with a host cron, scheduled function, or worker."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deploy the same workflow code on Cloudflare, Railway, Netlify, Node, Vercel,\nor your own infrastructure."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"package-map","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Package map"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Package"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Purpose"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-core"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The replay engine, workflow builder, primitives, middleware, version routing, and low-level "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-runtime"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The deployment-independent runtime, execution store contract, schedules, timers, leases, and sweep driver."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Postgres implementation of the runtime execution store contract using Drizzle as the SQL surface."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-store-cloudflare-d1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Cloudflare D1 implementation of the runtime execution store contract."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-cloudflare"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Cloudflare Worker "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scheduled()"},{"type":"text","value":" handler that calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runtime.sweep()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-railway"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Railway Cron Job command helper that calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runtime.sweep()"},{"type":"text","value":" and exits."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-netlify"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Netlify Scheduled Function handler that calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runtime.sweep()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-vercel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Vercel route handler that calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runtime.sweep()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The important boundary is this:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Workflow code is portable. Stores and host adapters are replaceable."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The engine is not Drizzle-backed, Vercel-backed, Netlify-backed, or cron-backed.\nThose are capability adapters around a common runtime/store contract."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"That is the main reason to reach for TanStack Workflow: you get durable\nexecution, but keep control over the runtime and persistence choices that make\nsense for your app."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"install","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Install"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For local development:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"pnpm add @tanstack/workflow-core @tanstack/workflow-runtime zod","lang":"bash"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a Postgres-backed deployment:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"pnpm add @tanstack/workflow-core @tanstack/workflow-runtime \\\n  @tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres drizzle-orm pg zod","lang":"bash"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add one host adapter when you deploy:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"pnpm add @tanstack/workflow-cloudflare\n# or\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-railway\n# or\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-netlify\n# or\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-vercel","lang":"bash"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"define-a-workflow","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Define a workflow"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Workflows are ordinary async functions. Side effects go through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.step"},{"type":"text","value":", and\npauses go through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.waitForEvent"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.approve"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.sleep"},{"type":"text","value":", or\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.sleepUntil"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nexport const fulfillmentWorkflow = createWorkflow({\n  id: 'fulfillment',\n  input: z.object({\n    orderId: z.string(),\n    delayMs: z.number(),\n  }),\n  output: z.object({\n    orderId: z.string(),\n    shipped: z.boolean(),\n  }),\n}).handler(async (ctx) => {\n  await ctx.step('reserve-inventory', async (stepCtx) => {\n    await reserveInventory(ctx.input.orderId, {\n      idempotencyKey: stepCtx.id,\n    })\n  })\n\n  const now = await ctx.now()\n  await ctx.sleepUntil(now + ctx.input.delayMs)\n\n  const payment = await ctx.waitForEvent<{ paymentId: string }>(\n    'payment-received',\n  )\n\n  await ctx.step('ship-order', async (stepCtx) => {\n    await shipOrder(ctx.input.orderId, payment.paymentId, {\n      idempotencyKey: stepCtx.id,\n    })\n  })\n\n  return {\n    orderId: ctx.input.orderId,\n    shipped: true,\n  }\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The workflow can pause for seconds, days, or months. It does not keep a function\ninvocation alive while it waits. The handler reaches a pause, persists its state,\nand returns. A later invocation resumes it."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"create-a-runtime","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Create a runtime"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The runtime registers workflows, owns schedules, and drives executions through a\ndurable execution store."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  defineWorkflowRuntime,\n  every,\n  inMemoryWorkflowExecutionStore,\n} from '@tanstack/workflow-runtime'\nimport { fulfillmentWorkflow } from './fulfillment'\n\nconst store = inMemoryWorkflowExecutionStore()\n\nexport const workflowRuntime = defineWorkflowRuntime({\n  store,\n  workflows: {\n    fulfillment: {\n      load: async () => fulfillmentWorkflow,\n      schedules: [\n        {\n          id: 'fulfillment-digest-every-15m',\n          schedule: every.minutes(15),\n          overlapPolicy: 'skip',\n          input: { batchSize: 100 },\n        },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the in-memory store for tests and demos only. Production deployments need a\nstore that can persist executions across invocations."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"add-opentelemetry","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add OpenTelemetry"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Workflow emits OpenTelemetry traces through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@opentelemetry/api"},{"type":"text","value":".\nApplications own SDK and exporter setup; Workflow emits no-op spans until an SDK\nis configured."}]},{"type":"code","value":"export const workflowRuntime = defineWorkflowRuntime({\n  store,\n  workflows,\n  telemetry: {\n    attributes: ({ workflowId }) => ({\n      'app.workflow': workflowId ?? 'unknown',\n    }),\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Workflow traces runtime operations, store calls, sweeps, and fresh "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.step"},{"type":"text","value":"\nexecutions. It does not record workflow input, output, signal payloads, step\nresults, or raw metadata as attributes by default."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"use-postgres-for-durability","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use Postgres for durability"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first production-style store is Drizzle/Postgres:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { drizzle } from 'drizzle-orm/node-postgres'\nimport { Pool } from 'pg'\nimport { createDrizzlePostgresWorkflowStore } from '@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres'\n\nconst db = drizzle(new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL }))\nconst store = createDrizzlePostgresWorkflowStore({ db })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Apply the package-owned Workflow store migration before runtime sweeps or\nrequests hit that database:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"psql \"$DATABASE_URL\" -f node_modules/@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres/migrations/0000_workflow_store.sql","lang":"bash"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Then pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"store"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineWorkflowRuntime"},{"type":"text","value":". The runtime will use it for:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"run creation and idempotency"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"append-only event logs"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"run state and pause metadata"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"timers"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"schedule buckets"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"signal and approval delivery"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"leases and stale run recovery"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Drizzle is the SQL execution surface. The workflow runtime is backed by the\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WorkflowExecutionStore"},{"type":"text","value":" contract."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"start-and-resume-runs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start and resume runs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runtime.startRun"},{"type":"text","value":" to start a new execution:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"await workflowRuntime.startRun({\n  workflowId: 'fulfillment',\n  runId: `fulfillment:${orderId}`,\n  input: { orderId, delayMs: 30_000 },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deliver external events with a stable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"signalId"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"await workflowRuntime.deliverSignal({\n  runId: `fulfillment:${orderId}`,\n  signalId: stripeEvent.id,\n  name: 'payment-received',\n  payload: { paymentId: stripeEvent.data.object.id },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deliver approvals the same way:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"await workflowRuntime.deliverApproval({\n  runId,\n  approval: {\n    approvalId,\n    approved: true,\n    feedback: 'Approved by finance',\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable IDs matter. Retries from Stripe, a webhook queue, or a user clicking twice\nshould use the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"signalId"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"approvalId"},{"type":"text","value":" so delivery is idempotent."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"wake-timers-and-schedules","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wake timers and schedules"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The runtime can sleep and schedule without owning a process. It uses a sweep:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"await workflowRuntime.sweep({\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  includeEvents: false,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A sweep does two jobs:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Claim due schedule buckets and start their workflow runs."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Claim due timers and deliver the internal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"__timer"},{"type":"text","value":" signal to paused runs."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The result is summary-first:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"{\n  scheduled: [],\n  timers: [],\n  summary: {\n    scheduled: { completed: 3 },\n    timers: { paused: 12 },\n    eventCount: 88,\n    returnedEventCount: 0,\n  },\n  deadlineReached: false,\n  remainingMayExist: false,\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, host adapters set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"includeEvents: false"},{"type":"text","value":" so a busy sweep does not\nretain every emitted event in memory. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"includeSweepResult"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"includeEvents"},{"type":"text","value":"\nonly when debugging."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"deploy-on-cloudflare","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deploy on Cloudflare"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare Workers can wake the runtime from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scheduled()"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createCloudflareWorkflowScheduledHandler } from '@tanstack/workflow-cloudflare'\n\nexport default {\n  scheduled: createCloudflareWorkflowScheduledHandler({\n    runtime: ({ env }) => createWorkflowRuntime(env),\n    maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n    maxTimers: 25,\n    maxDurationMs: 25_000,\n  }),\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Cron Trigger only wakes the runtime. The store decides what is actually due."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"deploy-on-railway","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deploy on Railway"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Railway Cron Jobs can run a small sweep command that exits after bounded work:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createRailwayWorkflowCronCommand } from '@tanstack/workflow-railway'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from './workflows/runtime.server'\n\nconst sweep = createRailwayWorkflowCronCommand({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  logSummary: true,\n})\n\nawait sweep()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configure the Railway service with config-as-code:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"# railway.toml\n[deploy]\nstartCommand = \"pnpm workflow:sweep\"\ncronSchedule = \"*/5 * * * *\"\nrestartPolicyType = \"NEVER\"","lang":"toml"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Railway runs the start command on the cron schedule. The command should exit\nafter the sweep finishes. Keep persistence in a durable store such as Postgres."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"deploy-on-netlify","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deploy on Netlify"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Create a Scheduled Function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// netlify/functions/workflow-sweep-background.ts\nimport {\n  createNetlifyWorkflowSweepHandler,\n} from '@tanstack/workflow-netlify'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from '../../src/workflows/runtime.server'\n\nexport default createNetlifyWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  maxDurationMs: 25_000,\n})\n\nexport const config = {\n  schedule: '*/5 * * * *',\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Scheduled Function only wakes the runtime. It does not store workflow state.\nApply the package-owned store migration during deploy/setup; do not mirror\nWorkflow's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"workflow_*"},{"type":"text","value":" tables in app schema files."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"deploy-on-vercel","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deploy on Vercel"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Create a route handler:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// app/api/workflow/sweep/route.ts\nimport { createVercelWorkflowSweepHandler } from '@tanstack/workflow-vercel'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from '@/workflows/runtime.server'\n\nexport const runtime = 'nodejs'\nexport const maxDuration = 60\n\nexport const GET = createVercelWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  cronSecret: process.env.CRON_SECRET,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configure Vercel Cron:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://openapi.vercel.sh/vercel.json\",\n  \"crons\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"/api/workflow/sweep\",\n      \"schedule\": \"*/5 * * * *\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","lang":"json"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Vercel Cron Job wakes the route. The database decides what is actually due.\nApply the package-owned store migration during deploy/setup; app code owns the\nworkflow definitions and route config, while Workflow owns its persistence\nschema."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-this-works-on-serverless-hosts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why this works on serverless hosts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every host invocation has a bounded responsibility:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"start a run and drive it to the next pause"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"deliver one signal or approval and drive to the next pause"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"sweep a bounded number of due timers and schedules"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No invocation has to outlive the host's function limit. Long-lived means the\nworkflow execution can span time. It does not mean one JavaScript process stays\nalive."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"production-checklist","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production checklist"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a durable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WorkflowExecutionStore"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use stable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"signalId"},{"type":"text","value":", approval IDs, and step IDs."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Put all side effects inside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.step"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configure host cron or scheduled functions to call a bounded sweep."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxDurationMs"},{"type":"text","value":" below the host function timeout."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"includeEvents: false"},{"type":"text","value":" for normal sweeps."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Treat "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"remainingMayExist: true"},{"type":"text","value":" as a signal to schedule or allow another\nsweep."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep previous workflow versions loadable until old runs finish."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add observability around sweep summaries, errors, and stale lease recovery."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"next","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"runtime-model.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Runtime model"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"persistence.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistence"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"deployment.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deployment"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../cookbook/index.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cookbook"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../api/index.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"API reference"}]}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"tutorial-and-guide","text":"Tutorial and guide","level":1},{"id":"package-map","text":"Package map","level":2},{"id":"install","text":"Install","level":2},{"id":"define-a-workflow","text":"Define a workflow","level":2},{"id":"create-a-runtime","text":"Create a runtime","level":2},{"id":"add-opentelemetry","text":"Add OpenTelemetry","level":2},{"id":"use-postgres-for-durability","text":"Use Postgres for durability","level":2},{"id":"start-and-resume-runs","text":"Start and resume runs","level":2},{"id":"wake-timers-and-schedules","text":"Wake timers and schedules","level":2},{"id":"deploy-on-cloudflare","text":"Deploy on Cloudflare","level":2},{"id":"deploy-on-railway","text":"Deploy on Railway","level":2},{"id":"deploy-on-netlify","text":"Deploy on Netlify","level":2},{"id":"deploy-on-vercel","text":"Deploy on Vercel","level":2},{"id":"why-this-works-on-serverless-hosts","text":"Why this works on serverless hosts","level":2},{"id":"production-checklist","text":"Production checklist","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n# Tutorial and guide\n\nTanStack Workflow is a headless durable execution system for TypeScript. You\nwrite workflows as ordinary async functions, mark durable work with explicit\nprimitives, and choose where persistence, timers, schedules, and deployment\nlive.\n\nThe production model is deliberately app-centered. The workflow engine does not\nbecome a separate platform where your business logic lives. Your app starts and\nresumes runs, your store records durability, and your host wakes bounded work\nwhen timers or schedules are due.\n\nThis guide walks through the production shape:\n\n1. Define a workflow with `@tanstack/workflow-core`.\n2. Put it behind a runtime with `@tanstack/workflow-runtime`.\n3. Persist executions in a durable store.\n4. Observe runtime, store, and step execution with OpenTelemetry.\n5. Wake due timers and schedules with a host cron, scheduled function, or worker.\n6. Deploy the same workflow code on Cloudflare, Railway, Netlify, Node, Vercel,\n   or your own infrastructure.\n\n## Package map\n\n| Package | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `@tanstack/workflow-core` | The replay engine, workflow builder, primitives, middleware, version routing, and low-level `RunStore`. |\n| `@tanstack/workflow-runtime` | The deployment-independent runtime, execution store contract, schedules, timers, leases, and sweep driver. |\n| `@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres` | A Postgres implementation of the runtime execution store contract using Drizzle as the SQL surface. |\n| `@tanstack/workflow-store-cloudflare-d1` | A Cloudflare D1 implementation of the runtime execution store contract. |\n| `@tanstack/workflow-cloudflare` | A Cloudflare Worker `scheduled()` handler that calls `runtime.sweep()`. |\n| `@tanstack/workflow-railway` | A Railway Cron Job command helper that calls `runtime.sweep()` and exits. |\n| `@tanstack/workflow-netlify` | A Netlify Scheduled Function handler that calls `runtime.sweep()`. |\n| `@tanstack/workflow-vercel` | A Vercel route handler that calls `runtime.sweep()`. |\n\nThe important boundary is this:\n\n> Workflow code is portable. Stores and host adapters are replaceable.\n\nThe engine is not Drizzle-backed, Vercel-backed, Netlify-backed, or cron-backed.\nThose are capability adapters around a common runtime/store contract.\n\nThat is the main reason to reach for TanStack Workflow: you get durable\nexecution, but keep control over the runtime and persistence choices that make\nsense for your app.\n\n## Install\n\nFor local development:\n\n```bash\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-core @tanstack/workflow-runtime zod\n```\n\nFor a Postgres-backed deployment:\n\n```bash\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-core @tanstack/workflow-runtime \\\n  @tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres drizzle-orm pg zod\n```\n\nAdd one host adapter when you deploy:\n\n```bash\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-cloudflare\n# or\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-railway\n# or\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-netlify\n# or\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-vercel\n```\n\n## Define a workflow\n\nWorkflows are ordinary async functions. Side effects go through `ctx.step`, and\npauses go through `ctx.waitForEvent`, `ctx.approve`, `ctx.sleep`, or\n`ctx.sleepUntil`.\n\n```ts\nimport { createWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nexport const fulfillmentWorkflow = createWorkflow({\n  id: 'fulfillment',\n  input: z.object({\n    orderId: z.string(),\n    delayMs: z.number(),\n  }),\n  output: z.object({\n    orderId: z.string(),\n    shipped: z.boolean(),\n  }),\n}).handler(async (ctx) => {\n  await ctx.step('reserve-inventory', async (stepCtx) => {\n    await reserveInventory(ctx.input.orderId, {\n      idempotencyKey: stepCtx.id,\n    })\n  })\n\n  const now = await ctx.now()\n  await ctx.sleepUntil(now + ctx.input.delayMs)\n\n  const payment = await ctx.waitForEvent<{ paymentId: string }>(\n    'payment-received',\n  )\n\n  await ctx.step('ship-order', async (stepCtx) => {\n    await shipOrder(ctx.input.orderId, payment.paymentId, {\n      idempotencyKey: stepCtx.id,\n    })\n  })\n\n  return {\n    orderId: ctx.input.orderId,\n    shipped: true,\n  }\n})\n```\n\nThe workflow can pause for seconds, days, or months. It does not keep a function\ninvocation alive while it waits. The handler reaches a pause, persists its state,\nand returns. A later invocation resumes it.\n\n## Create a runtime\n\nThe runtime registers workflows, owns schedules, and drives executions through a\ndurable execution store.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  defineWorkflowRuntime,\n  every,\n  inMemoryWorkflowExecutionStore,\n} from '@tanstack/workflow-runtime'\nimport { fulfillmentWorkflow } from './fulfillment'\n\nconst store = inMemoryWorkflowExecutionStore()\n\nexport const workflowRuntime = defineWorkflowRuntime({\n  store,\n  workflows: {\n    fulfillment: {\n      load: async () => fulfillmentWorkflow,\n      schedules: [\n        {\n          id: 'fulfillment-digest-every-15m',\n          schedule: every.minutes(15),\n          overlapPolicy: 'skip',\n          input: { batchSize: 100 },\n        },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nUse the in-memory store for tests and demos only. Production deployments need a\nstore that can persist executions across invocations.\n\n## Add OpenTelemetry\n\nTanStack Workflow emits OpenTelemetry traces through `@opentelemetry/api`.\nApplications own SDK and exporter setup; Workflow emits no-op spans until an SDK\nis configured.\n\n```ts\nexport const workflowRuntime = defineWorkflowRuntime({\n  store,\n  workflows,\n  telemetry: {\n    attributes: ({ workflowId }) => ({\n      'app.workflow': workflowId ?? 'unknown',\n    }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\nWorkflow traces runtime operations, store calls, sweeps, and fresh `ctx.step`\nexecutions. It does not record workflow input, output, signal payloads, step\nresults, or raw metadata as attributes by default.\n\n## Use Postgres for durability\n\nThe first production-style store is Drizzle/Postgres:\n\n```ts\nimport { drizzle } from 'drizzle-orm/node-postgres'\nimport { Pool } from 'pg'\nimport { createDrizzlePostgresWorkflowStore } from '@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres'\n\nconst db = drizzle(new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL }))\nconst store = createDrizzlePostgresWorkflowStore({ db })\n```\n\nApply the package-owned Workflow store migration before runtime sweeps or\nrequests hit that database:\n\n```bash\npsql \"$DATABASE_URL\" -f node_modules/@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres/migrations/0000_workflow_store.sql\n```\n\nThen pass `store` to `defineWorkflowRuntime`. The runtime will use it for:\n\n- run creation and idempotency\n- append-only event logs\n- run state and pause metadata\n- timers\n- schedule buckets\n- signal and approval delivery\n- leases and stale run recovery\n\nDrizzle is the SQL execution surface. The workflow runtime is backed by the\n`WorkflowExecutionStore` contract.\n\n## Start and resume runs\n\nUse `runtime.startRun` to start a new execution:\n\n```ts\nawait workflowRuntime.startRun({\n  workflowId: 'fulfillment',\n  runId: `fulfillment:${orderId}`,\n  input: { orderId, delayMs: 30_000 },\n})\n```\n\nDeliver external events with a stable `signalId`:\n\n```ts\nawait workflowRuntime.deliverSignal({\n  runId: `fulfillment:${orderId}`,\n  signalId: stripeEvent.id,\n  name: 'payment-received',\n  payload: { paymentId: stripeEvent.data.object.id },\n})\n```\n\nDeliver approvals the same way:\n\n```ts\nawait workflowRuntime.deliverApproval({\n  runId,\n  approval: {\n    approvalId,\n    approved: true,\n    feedback: 'Approved by finance',\n  },\n})\n```\n\nStable IDs matter. Retries from Stripe, a webhook queue, or a user clicking twice\nshould use the same `signalId` or `approvalId` so delivery is idempotent.\n\n## Wake timers and schedules\n\nThe runtime can sleep and schedule without owning a process. It uses a sweep:\n\n```ts\nawait workflowRuntime.sweep({\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  includeEvents: false,\n})\n```\n\nA sweep does two jobs:\n\n1. Claim due schedule buckets and start their workflow runs.\n2. Claim due timers and deliver the internal `__timer` signal to paused runs.\n\nThe result is summary-first:\n\n```ts\n{\n  scheduled: [],\n  timers: [],\n  summary: {\n    scheduled: { completed: 3 },\n    timers: { paused: 12 },\n    eventCount: 88,\n    returnedEventCount: 0,\n  },\n  deadlineReached: false,\n  remainingMayExist: false,\n}\n```\n\nBy default, host adapters set `includeEvents: false` so a busy sweep does not\nretain every emitted event in memory. Use `includeSweepResult` or `includeEvents`\nonly when debugging.\n\n## Deploy on Cloudflare\n\nCloudflare Workers can wake the runtime from `scheduled()`:\n\n```ts\nimport { createCloudflareWorkflowScheduledHandler } from '@tanstack/workflow-cloudflare'\n\nexport default {\n  scheduled: createCloudflareWorkflowScheduledHandler({\n    runtime: ({ env }) => createWorkflowRuntime(env),\n    maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n    maxTimers: 25,\n    maxDurationMs: 25_000,\n  }),\n}\n```\n\nThe Cron Trigger only wakes the runtime. The store decides what is actually due.\n\n## Deploy on Railway\n\nRailway Cron Jobs can run a small sweep command that exits after bounded work:\n\n```ts\nimport { createRailwayWorkflowCronCommand } from '@tanstack/workflow-railway'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from './workflows/runtime.server'\n\nconst sweep = createRailwayWorkflowCronCommand({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  logSummary: true,\n})\n\nawait sweep()\n```\n\nConfigure the Railway service with config-as-code:\n\n```toml\n# railway.toml\n[deploy]\nstartCommand = \"pnpm workflow:sweep\"\ncronSchedule = \"*/5 * * * *\"\nrestartPolicyType = \"NEVER\"\n```\n\nRailway runs the start command on the cron schedule. The command should exit\nafter the sweep finishes. Keep persistence in a durable store such as Postgres.\n\n## Deploy on Netlify\n\nCreate a Scheduled Function:\n\n```ts\n// netlify/functions/workflow-sweep-background.ts\nimport {\n  createNetlifyWorkflowSweepHandler,\n} from '@tanstack/workflow-netlify'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from '../../src/workflows/runtime.server'\n\nexport default createNetlifyWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  maxDurationMs: 25_000,\n})\n\nexport const config = {\n  schedule: '*/5 * * * *',\n}\n```\n\nThe Scheduled Function only wakes the runtime. It does not store workflow state.\nApply the package-owned store migration during deploy/setup; do not mirror\nWorkflow's `workflow_*` tables in app schema files.\n\n## Deploy on Vercel\n\nCreate a route handler:\n\n```ts\n// app/api/workflow/sweep/route.ts\nimport { createVercelWorkflowSweepHandler } from '@tanstack/workflow-vercel'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from '@/workflows/runtime.server'\n\nexport const runtime = 'nodejs'\nexport const maxDuration = 60\n\nexport const GET = createVercelWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  cronSecret: process.env.CRON_SECRET,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n})\n```\n\nConfigure Vercel Cron:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://openapi.vercel.sh/vercel.json\",\n  \"crons\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"/api/workflow/sweep\",\n      \"schedule\": \"*/5 * * * *\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe Vercel Cron Job wakes the route. The database decides what is actually due.\nApply the package-owned store migration during deploy/setup; app code owns the\nworkflow definitions and route config, while Workflow owns its persistence\nschema.\n\n## Why this works on serverless hosts\n\nEvery host invocation has a bounded responsibility:\n\n- start a run and drive it to the next pause\n- deliver one signal or approval and drive to the next pause\n- sweep a bounded number of due timers and schedules\n\nNo invocation has to outlive the host's function limit. Long-lived means the\nworkflow execution can span time. It does not mean one JavaScript process stays\nalive.\n\n## Production checklist\n\n- Use a durable `WorkflowExecutionStore`.\n- Use stable `runId`, `signalId`, approval IDs, and step IDs.\n- Put all side effects inside `ctx.step`.\n- Configure host cron or scheduled functions to call a bounded sweep.\n- Set `maxDurationMs` below the host function timeout.\n- Keep `includeEvents: false` for normal sweeps.\n- Treat `remainingMayExist: true` as a signal to schedule or allow another\n  sweep.\n- Keep previous workflow versions loadable until old runs finish.\n- Add observability around sweep summaries, errors, and stale lease recovery.\n\n## Next\n\n- [Runtime model](runtime-model.md)\n- [Persistence](persistence.md)\n- [Deployment](deployment.md)\n- [Cookbook](../cookbook/index.md)\n- [API reference](../api/index.md)\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: guide\ntitle: Tutorial and Guide\n---\n\n# Tutorial and guide\n\nTanStack Workflow is a headless durable execution system for TypeScript. You\nwrite workflows as ordinary async functions, mark durable work with explicit\nprimitives, and choose where persistence, timers, schedules, and deployment\nlive.\n\nThe production model is deliberately app-centered. The workflow engine does not\nbecome a separate platform where your business logic lives. Your app starts and\nresumes runs, your store records durability, and your host wakes bounded work\nwhen timers or schedules are due.\n\nThis guide walks through the production shape:\n\n1. Define a workflow with `@tanstack/workflow-core`.\n2. Put it behind a runtime with `@tanstack/workflow-runtime`.\n3. Persist executions in a durable store.\n4. Observe runtime, store, and step execution with OpenTelemetry.\n5. Wake due timers and schedules with a host cron, scheduled function, or worker.\n6. Deploy the same workflow code on Cloudflare, Railway, Netlify, Node, Vercel,\n   or your own infrastructure.\n\n## Package map\n\n| Package | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `@tanstack/workflow-core` | The replay engine, workflow builder, primitives, middleware, version routing, and low-level `RunStore`. |\n| `@tanstack/workflow-runtime` | The deployment-independent runtime, execution store contract, schedules, timers, leases, and sweep driver. |\n| `@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres` | A Postgres implementation of the runtime execution store contract using Drizzle as the SQL surface. |\n| `@tanstack/workflow-store-cloudflare-d1` | A Cloudflare D1 implementation of the runtime execution store contract. |\n| `@tanstack/workflow-cloudflare` | A Cloudflare Worker `scheduled()` handler that calls `runtime.sweep()`. |\n| `@tanstack/workflow-railway` | A Railway Cron Job command helper that calls `runtime.sweep()` and exits. |\n| `@tanstack/workflow-netlify` | A Netlify Scheduled Function handler that calls `runtime.sweep()`. |\n| `@tanstack/workflow-vercel` | A Vercel route handler that calls `runtime.sweep()`. |\n\nThe important boundary is this:\n\n> Workflow code is portable. Stores and host adapters are replaceable.\n\nThe engine is not Drizzle-backed, Vercel-backed, Netlify-backed, or cron-backed.\nThose are capability adapters around a common runtime/store contract.\n\nThat is the main reason to reach for TanStack Workflow: you get durable\nexecution, but keep control over the runtime and persistence choices that make\nsense for your app.\n\n## Install\n\nFor local development:\n\n```bash\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-core @tanstack/workflow-runtime zod\n```\n\nFor a Postgres-backed deployment:\n\n```bash\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-core @tanstack/workflow-runtime \\\n  @tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres drizzle-orm pg zod\n```\n\nAdd one host adapter when you deploy:\n\n```bash\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-cloudflare\n# or\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-railway\n# or\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-netlify\n# or\npnpm add @tanstack/workflow-vercel\n```\n\n## Define a workflow\n\nWorkflows are ordinary async functions. Side effects go through `ctx.step`, and\npauses go through `ctx.waitForEvent`, `ctx.approve`, `ctx.sleep`, or\n`ctx.sleepUntil`.\n\n```ts\nimport { createWorkflow } from '@tanstack/workflow-core'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nexport const fulfillmentWorkflow = createWorkflow({\n  id: 'fulfillment',\n  input: z.object({\n    orderId: z.string(),\n    delayMs: z.number(),\n  }),\n  output: z.object({\n    orderId: z.string(),\n    shipped: z.boolean(),\n  }),\n}).handler(async (ctx) => {\n  await ctx.step('reserve-inventory', async (stepCtx) => {\n    await reserveInventory(ctx.input.orderId, {\n      idempotencyKey: stepCtx.id,\n    })\n  })\n\n  const now = await ctx.now()\n  await ctx.sleepUntil(now + ctx.input.delayMs)\n\n  const payment = await ctx.waitForEvent<{ paymentId: string }>(\n    'payment-received',\n  )\n\n  await ctx.step('ship-order', async (stepCtx) => {\n    await shipOrder(ctx.input.orderId, payment.paymentId, {\n      idempotencyKey: stepCtx.id,\n    })\n  })\n\n  return {\n    orderId: ctx.input.orderId,\n    shipped: true,\n  }\n})\n```\n\nThe workflow can pause for seconds, days, or months. It does not keep a function\ninvocation alive while it waits. The handler reaches a pause, persists its state,\nand returns. A later invocation resumes it.\n\n## Create a runtime\n\nThe runtime registers workflows, owns schedules, and drives executions through a\ndurable execution store.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  defineWorkflowRuntime,\n  every,\n  inMemoryWorkflowExecutionStore,\n} from '@tanstack/workflow-runtime'\nimport { fulfillmentWorkflow } from './fulfillment'\n\nconst store = inMemoryWorkflowExecutionStore()\n\nexport const workflowRuntime = defineWorkflowRuntime({\n  store,\n  workflows: {\n    fulfillment: {\n      load: async () => fulfillmentWorkflow,\n      schedules: [\n        {\n          id: 'fulfillment-digest-every-15m',\n          schedule: every.minutes(15),\n          overlapPolicy: 'skip',\n          input: { batchSize: 100 },\n        },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nUse the in-memory store for tests and demos only. Production deployments need a\nstore that can persist executions across invocations.\n\n## Add OpenTelemetry\n\nTanStack Workflow emits OpenTelemetry traces through `@opentelemetry/api`.\nApplications own SDK and exporter setup; Workflow emits no-op spans until an SDK\nis configured.\n\n```ts\nexport const workflowRuntime = defineWorkflowRuntime({\n  store,\n  workflows,\n  telemetry: {\n    attributes: ({ workflowId }) => ({\n      'app.workflow': workflowId ?? 'unknown',\n    }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\nWorkflow traces runtime operations, store calls, sweeps, and fresh `ctx.step`\nexecutions. It does not record workflow input, output, signal payloads, step\nresults, or raw metadata as attributes by default.\n\n## Use Postgres for durability\n\nThe first production-style store is Drizzle/Postgres:\n\n```ts\nimport { drizzle } from 'drizzle-orm/node-postgres'\nimport { Pool } from 'pg'\nimport { createDrizzlePostgresWorkflowStore } from '@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres'\n\nconst db = drizzle(new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL }))\nconst store = createDrizzlePostgresWorkflowStore({ db })\n```\n\nApply the package-owned Workflow store migration before runtime sweeps or\nrequests hit that database:\n\n```bash\npsql \"$DATABASE_URL\" -f node_modules/@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres/migrations/0000_workflow_store.sql\n```\n\nThen pass `store` to `defineWorkflowRuntime`. The runtime will use it for:\n\n- run creation and idempotency\n- append-only event logs\n- run state and pause metadata\n- timers\n- schedule buckets\n- signal and approval delivery\n- leases and stale run recovery\n\nDrizzle is the SQL execution surface. The workflow runtime is backed by the\n`WorkflowExecutionStore` contract.\n\n## Start and resume runs\n\nUse `runtime.startRun` to start a new execution:\n\n```ts\nawait workflowRuntime.startRun({\n  workflowId: 'fulfillment',\n  runId: `fulfillment:${orderId}`,\n  input: { orderId, delayMs: 30_000 },\n})\n```\n\nDeliver external events with a stable `signalId`:\n\n```ts\nawait workflowRuntime.deliverSignal({\n  runId: `fulfillment:${orderId}`,\n  signalId: stripeEvent.id,\n  name: 'payment-received',\n  payload: { paymentId: stripeEvent.data.object.id },\n})\n```\n\nDeliver approvals the same way:\n\n```ts\nawait workflowRuntime.deliverApproval({\n  runId,\n  approval: {\n    approvalId,\n    approved: true,\n    feedback: 'Approved by finance',\n  },\n})\n```\n\nStable IDs matter. Retries from Stripe, a webhook queue, or a user clicking twice\nshould use the same `signalId` or `approvalId` so delivery is idempotent.\n\n## Wake timers and schedules\n\nThe runtime can sleep and schedule without owning a process. It uses a sweep:\n\n```ts\nawait workflowRuntime.sweep({\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  includeEvents: false,\n})\n```\n\nA sweep does two jobs:\n\n1. Claim due schedule buckets and start their workflow runs.\n2. Claim due timers and deliver the internal `__timer` signal to paused runs.\n\nThe result is summary-first:\n\n```ts\n{\n  scheduled: [],\n  timers: [],\n  summary: {\n    scheduled: { completed: 3 },\n    timers: { paused: 12 },\n    eventCount: 88,\n    returnedEventCount: 0,\n  },\n  deadlineReached: false,\n  remainingMayExist: false,\n}\n```\n\nBy default, host adapters set `includeEvents: false` so a busy sweep does not\nretain every emitted event in memory. Use `includeSweepResult` or `includeEvents`\nonly when debugging.\n\n## Deploy on Cloudflare\n\nCloudflare Workers can wake the runtime from `scheduled()`:\n\n```ts\nimport { createCloudflareWorkflowScheduledHandler } from '@tanstack/workflow-cloudflare'\n\nexport default {\n  scheduled: createCloudflareWorkflowScheduledHandler({\n    runtime: ({ env }) => createWorkflowRuntime(env),\n    maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n    maxTimers: 25,\n    maxDurationMs: 25_000,\n  }),\n}\n```\n\nThe Cron Trigger only wakes the runtime. The store decides what is actually due.\n\n## Deploy on Railway\n\nRailway Cron Jobs can run a small sweep command that exits after bounded work:\n\n```ts\nimport { createRailwayWorkflowCronCommand } from '@tanstack/workflow-railway'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from './workflows/runtime.server'\n\nconst sweep = createRailwayWorkflowCronCommand({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  logSummary: true,\n})\n\nawait sweep()\n```\n\nConfigure the Railway service with config-as-code:\n\n```toml\n# railway.toml\n[deploy]\nstartCommand = \"pnpm workflow:sweep\"\ncronSchedule = \"*/5 * * * *\"\nrestartPolicyType = \"NEVER\"\n```\n\nRailway runs the start command on the cron schedule. The command should exit\nafter the sweep finishes. Keep persistence in a durable store such as Postgres.\n\n## Deploy on Netlify\n\nCreate a Scheduled Function:\n\n```ts\n// netlify/functions/workflow-sweep-background.ts\nimport {\n  createNetlifyWorkflowSweepHandler,\n} from '@tanstack/workflow-netlify'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from '../../src/workflows/runtime.server'\n\nexport default createNetlifyWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  maxDurationMs: 25_000,\n})\n\nexport const config = {\n  schedule: '*/5 * * * *',\n}\n```\n\nThe Scheduled Function only wakes the runtime. It does not store workflow state.\nApply the package-owned store migration during deploy/setup; do not mirror\nWorkflow's `workflow_*` tables in app schema files.\n\n## Deploy on Vercel\n\nCreate a route handler:\n\n```ts\n// app/api/workflow/sweep/route.ts\nimport { createVercelWorkflowSweepHandler } from '@tanstack/workflow-vercel'\nimport { workflowRuntime } from '@/workflows/runtime.server'\n\nexport const runtime = 'nodejs'\nexport const maxDuration = 60\n\nexport const GET = createVercelWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  cronSecret: process.env.CRON_SECRET,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n})\n```\n\nConfigure Vercel Cron:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://openapi.vercel.sh/vercel.json\",\n  \"crons\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"/api/workflow/sweep\",\n      \"schedule\": \"*/5 * * * *\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe Vercel Cron Job wakes the route. The database decides what is actually due.\nApply the package-owned store migration during deploy/setup; app code owns the\nworkflow definitions and route config, while Workflow owns its persistence\nschema.\n\n## Why this works on serverless hosts\n\nEvery host invocation has a bounded responsibility:\n\n- start a run and drive it to the next pause\n- deliver one signal or approval and drive to the next pause\n- sweep a bounded number of due timers and schedules\n\nNo invocation has to outlive the host's function limit. Long-lived means the\nworkflow execution can span time. It does not mean one JavaScript process stays\nalive.\n\n## Production checklist\n\n- Use a durable `WorkflowExecutionStore`.\n- Use stable `runId`, `signalId`, approval IDs, and step IDs.\n- Put all side effects inside `ctx.step`.\n- Configure host cron or scheduled functions to call a bounded sweep.\n- Set `maxDurationMs` below the host function timeout.\n- Keep `includeEvents: false` for normal sweeps.\n- Treat `remainingMayExist: true` as a signal to schedule or allow another\n  sweep.\n- Keep previous workflow versions loadable until old runs finish.\n- Add observability around sweep summaries, errors, and stale lease recovery.\n\n## Next\n\n- [Runtime model](runtime-model.md)\n- [Persistence](persistence.md)\n- [Deployment](deployment.md)\n- [Cookbook](../cookbook/index.md)\n- [API reference](../api/index.md)\n"}
